#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
smc3_scope v2.8 - live scope, tuner and auto-tuner for the SMC3_R4 motion controller (Arduino UNO R4 WiFi).

Connects to the board's TCP telemetry port, keeps a rolling buffer of samples and serves a web page with live
charts, a parameter editor (sends ordinary SMC3 commands back to the board), test signals and a per-motor
auto-tune.  SimHub keeps the USB port; this runs alongside it.  For calibration with SimHub closed the page can use
the USB cable instead (firmware 1.9.5): a bridge on the PC with the cable links it -

    python3 smc3_scope.py --usb-bridge COM5 --page http://<page address>:<port>

or, on Windows with nothing installed, the PowerShell bridge from Settings, USB cable.

    python3 smc3_scope.py --host 192.168.1.50 --bind 0.0.0.0 --web 8198

Every option can also come from the environment (Docker): SMC3_HOST, SMC3_PORT, SMC3_WEB_PORT, SMC3_BIND,
SMC3_DATA_DIR.  Settings changed in the page (board address, theme, auto-find) are saved to
<data dir>/config.json and survive restarts and upgrades.  Standard-library Python 3.8+ only.

v2.8: the USB cable for calibration (firmware 1.9.5). The board's WiFi module now and then holds the board's loop up
      for a second or more, so the page's moves lag and auto-tune redoes test moves. With SimHub closed, a bridge on
      the PC the board is plugged into links the page to the board's USB port: Settings, USB cable has the one line to
      paste into Win+R (a PowerShell bridge, nothing to install; or smc3_scope.py --usb-bridge COM5 --page URL). The
      bridge connects out to the page (POST /usb/link, then raw bytes both ways); the page takes the link over from
      WiFi, sends [kU1] every second, and the board streams its telemetry over USB and makes no WiFi calls at all.
      Closing the bridge (or Back to WiFi) sends [kU0]: the board and the page go back to WiFi by themselves. The
      header says USB cable or WiFi. A bridge waits while auto-tune, the full check, Seat setup or a firmware update
      runs; a board without 1.9.5 is noticed (it answers but doesn't stream) and the bridge told so. Firmware updates
      stay WiFi only. Back to WiFi waits for a running auto-tune or check; a new connection during auto-tune ends it and
      the previous settings go back to the board (at once, or once it is reachable again). Simulator: --usb PATH (a
      pseudo-terminal), --fw194, --usb-reset SEC.
v2.7.3: Update page no longer refuses while the page holds the seat (Return to center, a dragged knob) or a test signal
        runs: just before the restart it gives the seat back to SimHub (the motors stay where they are) and stops the
        test signal. It still waits for auto-tune, the full check, Seat setup and a firmware update, which change the
        board's settings for a while.
v2.7.2: auto-tune and the full check cope with WiFi stalls (the R4's WiFi module blocks the board's loop for a second
        or more now and then): a test move the stall spoiled - readings missing, or targets that reached the board
        bunched up so the ramp was really a jump - is done again (up to three times) instead of stopping auto-tune
        with "couldn't see the move" or measuring the WiFi's delay as the motor's lag; targets the page sent in the
        last 15 s count as its own (2.7.1 allowed 2 s, so targets delivered late looked like SimHub: "something else
        is moving motor 3"); and on firmware 1.9 SimHub's positions wait while auto-tune or the full check moves a
        motor ([kH], as in Seat setup), so SimHub can't get in the way at all. Simulator: --stalls SEC.
v2.7.1: Help has a drawing of the whole rig's wiring (the board, three IBT-2 bridges, motors with pots, the motion
        sensor, the motor supply) and the motion sensor's own drawing. A newer page or firmware on GitHub shows as a
        balloon on the header's Settings button (it leads to Settings, Updates); the header shows the page's version
        next to the firmware's. Quick Controls' Return to center, Resume SimHub, Read from board and Save to EEPROM
        wrap their text instead of running over the button's edges on a narrow card.
v2.7: updates from GitHub. The page asks the repository's latest release twice a day (Settings, Updates; or Check now)
      and says when there is a newer page or firmware - in the header too. Update page downloads the new page into
      <data dir>/updates, checks it (GitHub's SHA-256, its version, that it compiles) and restarts into it; the file
      you started (in Docker the image's) hands over to it at every start while it is newer, so the update survives
      restarts and re-created containers; one that doesn't start, or stops three times in a row within 10 minutes,
      is dropped (SMC3_NO_UPDATE=1 skips it). Go back to built-in undoes it. A restart waits until nothing moves the
      motors; on Windows the started file stays as the page's parent. Firmware is offered as a download only (the
      .ino, to flash with the Arduino IDE or export as .bin for Settings, Firmware update).
v2.6.2: nothing changes in what the page does. A step-by-step install guide (docs/INSTALL.md) for Windows, Mac, Linux,
        Raspberry Pi, Docker and Synology Container Manager, and a ready-made Docker image (ghcr.io/<owner>/smc3-scope,
        for amd64, arm64 and arm/v7) that GitHub Actions builds for every release tag; scope/compose.prebuilt.yaml uses
        it.
v2.6.1: auto-tune on a real rig (the first run of 2.6) settled for a soft Kp and far too much Ki. "Falls behind" is
        judged where the goal moves at full speed and only with the drive at PWMmax - 2.5's test (the last quarter of
        the move) is all easing-out on 2.6's shorter moves, so it flagged most Kp values and the sweep fell back to Kp
        149 x 90% although Kp 251 met the lag target both ways. Ki stops once a step no longer brings the leftover error
        down by a count (it's at the floor the dead zone and pot noise set), and the smallest Ki within a count of the
        best is kept (it went to Ki 70 for 2 counts less error and 25 points more overshoot on small steps). Diagnostics
        warns, and auto-tune's log says, when your answer to the full check's question disagrees with how that corner
        sinks under the seat's weight.
v2.6: auto-tune moves out and back from the start position, the way you move a knob on the dashboard: up (or right) by
      the test travel, back to the start, down (left), back to the start - so the seat never goes further than that from
      where it was, each direction is tested going away from the start and coming back, and a test no longer starts by
      going down (2.5 went from the bottom of the travel to the top and back). Small steps are +-30 counts from the
      start; the top speed is timed on two equal moves (down and back up over twice the travel); a short travel still
      gets a gradual move (at least 0.3 s). The sweep charts scale to the results (2.5's overshoot chart ran up to the
      limit line, so 0-2 % looked flat) and switch between overshoot, off by and settle time. The full check notices
      which way a lift's numbers run when its corner sinks (drift with the motor off, or sinking at low drive): auto-
      tune says up and down from that until you answer the question. Diagnostics warns when the two front lifts seem to
      run opposite ways (from that, or from needing clearly less drive one way; compared in SimHub's numbers, so
      REVERSE_MOTOR1 is allowed for). Pot glitches: firmware 1.9.4 holds a glitch back from the PID for up to 10 ms (the
      board still counts them; build flag bit 5 says the filter is in), and Help, Pot noise filter has the drawing and
      parts of an RC filter (1 kOhm, 100 nF, 1 MOhm per pot) and the wiring rules; the glitch and noise findings point
      there. Diagnostics reports the board's own firmware version (1.9.3 showed as 1.9.2). Sweep moves hold up to 2 s on
      a slow motor (1.3 s cut a slow motor's sweep short at Kp 40), a motor still creeping in isn't called too lively,
      and the wrong-way stop needs it to last 150 ms longer, so a cluster of glitchy readings can't fake it. Simulator:
      firmware 1.9.4 with its filter, also after an update from the page (--no-potfilter for 1.9.3), --sag- high.
v2.5: auto-tune tests each direction on its own (up and down for a lift, left and right for the rear slide) and
      reports both; after measuring the motor's top speed each way (one quick move), every test is a gradual move at
      60% of the slower way's top speed, so the motor can follow it and Kp is judged on how closely it follows (2.4.1
      jumped the target, so every test ran the motor flat out). "Who's in the seat": nobody, with the rider's weight
      allowed for (stricter overshoot, a stiffer Kp, slower tests, a little Ki), or you, after a 15 s countdown.
      Check tests the current values (typically with you in the seat) and says what to change. A stop holds the
      motor where it is (the median of its last readings), a refusal sends nothing, and the wrong-way watch rests
      while you get in. Small test steps are
      30 counts each way (were 15 up, 30 down). A glitchy pot can't spoil a tune: pot readings are cleaned of glitches
      (each compared with the median of the 11 around it) before overshoot, settling, ringing, a wrong-way start or
      running away are judged, the Kp sweep stops on overshoot only twice in a row, and a Kp spoiled right after a
      calm one is tested again. Auto-tune live: gauges for each test (both directions as needles),
      the minimum drive, top speed, and the Kp sweep as two small charts. Copy puts the log on the clipboard (also on
      plain http). Space or Esc stops auto-tune. The full check remembers which way each lift goes, so auto-tune can
      say up and down. Simulator: --load KG (a rider), --vmax.
v2.4.1: a pot whose wiper loses contact now and then (readings 20-40 counts off, sometimes a few in a row) no longer
      fakes a fault: the full check's crosstalk test ("motors mixed up") looks at an 11-reading median and its own
      checks at 7, from the first reading on; moving a motor from the page judges it on 5-reading medians; the
      watcher's "swinging around its target" needs median-filtered swings around the target itself. Moving a motor
      from the page: one that runs away fast (wired backwards) is switched off as before, but one that sinks slowly,
      is stuck or falls behind is now held where it is (switching it off would let a seat that sinks sink further).
      "Moved away from its target while driven" no longer says to swap motor wires when the full check found the
      motor goes the right way. The full check calls a motor "WRONG way" only when it goes the wrong way both ways
      (one that sinks under its load at low drive is retried with more drive and reported as sinking), and keeps
      watching the motor it is about to test while it measures how the others drift. Old WiFi module: step-by-step (close SimHub, Firmware Updater, then upload SMC3_R4
      again - the updater erases the sketch). Simulator: --pot-glitches M:N.
v2.4: move the motors from the page (firmware 1.9): drag a motor card's knob and the motor follows (up to 200
      counts/s, easing in and out, inside its Clip window and travel limits); Quick Controls, Return to center
      brings every motor back to its neutral position (SimHub's 0 - the level seat) and Resume SimHub gives the seat
      back. Meanwhile the board ignores SimHub ([kH], as Seat setup does) and runs at full power. Auto-tune copes with
      a noisy pot and a seat that sinks under its weight: movement is judged on the median of several readings
      against limits above the pot's noise (2.3.1 compared two single readings with a 6-count limit, so a +-2-count
      pot read as "moved the wrong way"), "wired backwards" needs the wrong way BOTH ways, and the "holds still" and
      ringing limits grow with the noise. A safety stop now switches the motor off with its own settings back
      (2.3.1 left it on with PWMmax 0). The page re-reads the PID table after any auto-tune ends. The full check
      and the watcher use the same median-based wrong-way test.
v2.3.1: ships with firmware 1.9.3 - 1.9.2 didn't compile in the Arduino IDE (the boards package's GCC 7). The page
      names SMC3_R4_v1.9.3.ino where it tells you what to flash; nothing else changed.
v2.3: firmware update over WiFi (firmware 1.9.2): Settings, Firmware update - pick the .bin the Arduino IDE exports
      (Sketch, Export Compiled Binary); the page checks it, converts it to the UNO R4 WiFi's .ota format and serves
      it, the board's WiFi module downloads, checks and installs it, and the page confirms the new version. The
      board also remembers its WiFi name and password, so builds with the placeholders keep it on the network.
v2.2: soft start (firmware 1.9.1): the motors creep to their positions after power-up and Enable Motors.
v2.1: traction-loss rigs: motor 3 slides the rear about a front pivot; Seat setup, diagnostics and the 3D view follow.
v2.0: firmware 1.9 support. Seat setup (new section): level the platform first (by itself with the motion sensor),
      then make that SimHub's centre; find each motor's safe travel both ways (it stops at the angle you allow, when
      it pushes against something, or when you press Stop); then save travel limits, end stops and tilt limits for
      each direction on the board. 3D View (new section): a sci-fi cockpit seat moving live with the sensor and the
      motors, with the sensor's angles, gyro and acceleration. Motion sensor: tilt limits per direction, set-up
      with gyro + acceleration. Auto-tune and the full check stay inside the travel limits.
v1.9.1: same page as 1.9, shipped with firmware 1.8.1 (1.8 didn't compile in the Arduino IDE).
v1.9: firmware 1.8 support - LED matrix settings (motor bars / seat horizon / quiet, idle trace) and the WT901C
      motion sensor (Settings): seat angle chart, levelling, mounting, tilt-limit safety cut-off, set to 100 Hz.
      Diagnostics: sensor findings, the seat's own response in the full check (degrees per count, bounce, play),
      and a load map while SimHub drives (where a motor falls behind or runs at full power, each way). Auto-tune
      watches the seat too when the sensor sends. Help explains every LED matrix error code.
v1.8.1: single-sample pot glitches no longer distort step measurements (full check, auto-tune); wider
      test-signal motor menu; a browser closing mid-request no longer prints a traceback.
v1.8: motor names (Settings): call the motors by where they sit (Left, Right, Rear, ... or your own words);
      the names show on the cards, graphs, PID table, menus, auto-tune and diagnostics, next to the number.
v1.7: Diagnostics (Settings section): a quick check (no movement) and a full check that moves each motor a
      little - direction, wiring swaps, drive needed, step response, and what you saw - plus an event log and a
      watcher that notices problems while you drive (pot glitches, a motor moving away from its target or
      stalling, hunting, cutoff trips, board restarts) and keeps a few seconds of data around each. Everything
      goes into one file to hand to whoever helps you. Firmware 1.7 adds reset cause, SimHub USB counters, pot
      glitches at 4 kHz, why a motor is off and an EEPROM copy.
v1.6: EEPROM backups kept on the NAS (every save and every bigger change) with one-click switch back,
      recommended starting points per sim type (circuit, rally, flight, space, trucks), selected-motor
      highlight across the page, live-graph expand/collapse, motors-in-use setting (1-3), target smoothing
      and max speed as settings (firmware 1.6), favicon.
v1.5: new dashboard look (sidebar sections: Dashboard, Tuning, Motors, Live Graphs, Test & Tools, Profiles,
      Auto-Tune, EEPROM, Settings, Help), motor cards, quick controls incl. Disable Motors (firmware 1.5),
      test-signal presets and offset, named profiles stored next to config.json, EEPROM backup download /
      restore, Day / Dark / Auto theme.
v1.4: settings dialog (board address, port, find-board scan, auto-find), day/night theme, hover help on every
      setting, per-motor auto-tune; Kp/Ki/Kd shown in SMC3Utils units (raw numbers, e.g. Kp 300);
      fixed a thread race that made the page flash "not reachable".
"""

